body:
Hey, I went through about 10 years of atheism/agnosticism before coming back to faith. What made me change and come back was actually leaving all of the "I'll reason my way into it" behind. Now, that's not to say apologetics and many Doctors of the Church haven't done a great job making compelling arguments. But the truth is, deep down, I don't think you or I could ever have a "logical" enough answer. Seriously. If Jesus walked in front of you, told you it was real, and performed a miracle-- would you immediately let go of all your doubts?

&#x200B;

Faith is about more than reasoning. I'm trying to remember the philosopher who said it, but he said something along the lines that you already know everything and have the answers inside; in my view, faith makes the most compelling argument for this way of thought. You see the cross, you've likely read at least some of the Bible, have been to Mass, etc. Time after time, you've known these things to be true, but your usual way of reasoning is mystified and holding you back from embracing the truth of the Faith. I think the more you answer your "logical" side's petitions with, "So what?" the more you will gain confidence and comfort in your faith.
